@@ -0,0 +1,359 @@
+# Independent adversarial review — 84-solve
+
+## Outcome
+
+The candidate contains a legitimate, result-constraining partial-correctness
+proof of the submitted program for every integer `N` in the stated domain
+`0 <= N <= 10000`. Fresh LLVM and Haskell builds succeeded, the sole target
+claim independently returned `#Top` with exit 0, the formal claim embeds the
+actual submitted `solution.mpy`, and a fresh false-result mutation was rejected
+at the expected obligation.
+
+The result is `CONCERNS / LEGIT`, rather than `PASS`, for two auditability
+limitations: four required provenance artifacts are missing, and the candidate
+does not include a bridge-free universal connection theorem for its symbolic
+prefix-slice acceleration. The rule is nevertheless an exact slice identity:
+there is no false-conclusion witness, ordinary structural reasoning validates
+it for every finite `IntSeq`, and bridge-free K proofs cover all 37 payloads
+reachable from the intended domain with arbitrary continuations.
+
+## 1. Input and provenance integrity
+
+The rendered mode is `SUPPLIED_SEMANTICS`. The trusted mount is consistent with
+that mode: `/reference/reference-semantics` exists. There is no infrastructure
+breach.
+
+The reviewer read or checked the requested candidate-side artifacts only as
+untrusted evidence. These required provenance files are absent:
+
+- `/candidate/run-input.json`
+- `/candidate/metrics.json`
+- `/candidate/codex-last.txt`
+- `/candidate/codex-output.log`
+
+No structured generation trace is present. Because the files do not exist,
+their claims could not be inspected. This is a candidate provenance defect, not
+an infrastructure failure.
+
+Integrity checks found:
+
+- candidate `prompt.py` is byte-identical to `/reference/prompt.py`;
+- candidate `py2mpy.py` is byte-identical to `/reference/py2mpy.py`;
+- recursive `diff -r --no-dereference` between the trusted and candidate
+  `reference-semantics/` trees exits 0;
+- neither semantics tree contains a symlink;
+- all candidate proof/program source artifacts are ordinary files;
+- the candidate tree contains non-authoritative `prove.sh`, concrete tests,
+  and Python bytecode caches, but no extra entry inside the protected
+  `reference-semantics/` tree.
+
+Exact checks, hashes, inventories, and exit statuses are in
+[`evidence/01_integrity.log`](evidence/01_integrity.log), generated by
+[`evidence/01_integrity.sh`](evidence/01_integrity.sh). The source-only scratch
+copy procedure is preserved in
+[`evidence/00_prepare_scratch.sh`](evidence/00_prepare_scratch.sh) and
+[`evidence/00_prepare_scratch.log`](evidence/00_prepare_scratch.log). No
+candidate compiled definition or cache was copied into the proof inputs.
+
+Stage result: integrity of the trusted prompt, translator, and supplied
+semantics passes; provenance completeness has concerns.
+
+## 2. Program fidelity and candidate-versus-canonical checks
+
+### Contract
+
+For an integer `N` with `0 <= N <= 10000`, sum the decimal digits of `N` and
+return the ordinary base-2 numeral for that sum as a string, with no `0b`
+prefix. The documented examples are:
+
+- `1000 -> "1"`
+- `150 -> "110"`
+- `147 -> "1100"`
+
+Although the prose says “positive,” its explicit constraint includes zero; the
+audited domain therefore includes `N=0`, whose result is `"0"`.
+
+### Implementation
+
+`solution.py` adds the units, tens, hundreds, thousands, and ten-thousands
+digits with `%` and `//`, then returns `bin(digit_sum)[2:]`. Those five places
+cover the entire stated domain, including the endpoint 10000. The algorithm may
+disagree outside that domain, but out-of-domain behavior is not claimed.
+
+The trusted translator command
+
+`python3 /reference/py2mpy.py /tmp/audit-work/candidate-src/solution.py > /tmp/audit-work/regenerated-solution.mpy`
+
+exits 0. `cmp` against the submitted `solution.mpy` exits 0, and both files have
+SHA-256
+`1e93ee0cd84b92e349b2034c53e7ab1045b7ce07f79bf3d959013a4bd312d5eb`.
+
+The independent differential script imports the trusted canonical entry point
+and scratch-copied candidate entry point. It checks the documented examples,
+decimal-place boundaries, 128 seeded representative inputs, and exhaustively
+checks every integer from 0 through 10000. An “empty” value is inapplicable to
+an integer argument; zero is the lower-boundary case. The exact command
+
+`python3 /audit-output/evidence/02_differential.py`
+
+exits 0 with `mismatch_count=0` over 10,001 inputs. The deterministic result-row
+digest is
+`2932c84a56dc96394479ae67de8c0b81530b967e3a172662f785dbf50f3faa8e`.
+
+Artifacts:
+
+- [`evidence/02_differential.py`](evidence/02_differential.py)
+- [`evidence/02_run_fidelity.sh`](evidence/02_run_fidelity.sh)
+- [`evidence/02_fidelity.log`](evidence/02_fidelity.log)
+
+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 3. Clean proof reconstruction
+
+K 7.1.337 was used. All outputs were created under the scratch source tree;
+the pre-build checks confirmed that neither output definition existed.
+
+The reviewer-authored reconstruction ran:
+
+1. `kompile .../reference-semantics/semantics.k --backend llvm --main-module MPY-KRUN --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX --output-definition .../runtime-kompiled` — exit 0.
+2. `krun .../concrete-tests.mpy --definition .../runtime-kompiled --output pretty` — exit 0, final `<k> .K </k>`, `<exc> NoExc </exc>`, and `<exit-code> 0 </exit-code>`. The harness covers 0, all three examples, 9999, and 10000.
+3. `kompile .../verification.k --backend haskell --main-module VERIFICATION --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX --output-definition .../verification-kompiled` — exit 0.
+4. `kprove .../spec.k --definition .../verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC --output pretty` — exit 0 and `#Top`.
+
+`spec.k` contains exactly one positive target claim, so every positive target
+was rerun. Compiler warnings concern unused variables and incomplete totals in
+unrelated supplied-semantic helpers; they do not affect command success or the
+used program path.
+
+Exact commands and bounded output:
+
+- [`evidence/03_rebuild_and_prove.sh`](evidence/03_rebuild_and_prove.sh)
+- [`evidence/03_rebuild_and_prove.log`](evidence/03_rebuild_and_prove.log)
+
+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 4. Adequacy and real-program pinning
+
+### Entry claim in plain language
+
+Precondition:
+
+- `N` is a K integer;
+- `0 <= N <= 10000`;
+- execution begins with `#loadAll(the submitted module) ~> solve(N)`;
+- environment 0 is active;
+- module scope 0 is empty with builtins parent `-1`;
+- the supplied builtins scope exists;
+- heap and stack are empty, allocation counters are initial, return state is
+  `noRet`, and exception state is `NoExc`;
+- the omitted exit-code cell is framed and preserved. A concrete satisfying
+  instance has exit code 0.
+
+Postcondition:
+
+- the `<k>` result is exactly
+  `binaryNumeral(decimalDigitSum(N))`;
+- module scope contains the exact loaded `solve` closure;
+- the caller environment, allocation counters, empty heap/stack, return state,
+  and exception state are restored/preserved.
+
+The payload inside `#loadAll(...)` is whitespace-normalized identical to the
+submitted `solution.mpy`; the check exits 0 with
+`whitespace_normalized_identity=True`. See
+[`evidence/04_pinning.py`](evidence/04_pinning.py) and
+[`evidence/04_pinning.log`](evidence/04_pinning.log).
+
+The actual body is executed through ordinary supplied rules for definition
+loading, name lookup, argument evaluation, closure invocation, parameter
+binding, assignment, arithmetic, builtin dispatch, return, and frame pop. There
+is no helper or loop claim and no rule intercepts the `solve` call. The only
+execution acceleration is the exact prefix-slice rule reviewed in stage 5.
+
+The returned value is not a free variable, oracle, one-way implication, or
+tautology. It is fixed to the binary encoding of the five explicitly defined
+digits. Satisfying substitutions agree with both Python implementations:
+
+| `N` | digit sum | claimed/canonical/candidate result |
+|---:|---:|---|
+| 0 | 0 | `"0"` |
+| 147 | 12 | `"1100"` |
+| 150 | 6 | `"110"` |
+| 1000 | 1 | `"1"` |
+| 9999 | 36 | `"100100"` |
+| 10000 | 1 | `"1"` |
+
+The proof-summary equations were independently checked against a decimal-string
+oracle for all 10,001 domain integers with zero mismatches:
+[`evidence/05_summary_math.py`](evidence/05_summary_math.py) and
+[`evidence/05_summary_math.log`](evidence/05_summary_math.log).
+
+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 5. Rule-by-rule static soundness review
+
+### Exhaustive inventory
+
+[`evidence/05_rule_inventory.md`](evidence/05_rule_inventory.md) exhaustively
+enumerates every top-level item in the supplied `semantics.k`, every helper K
+file, `verification.k`, and `spec.k`:
+
+- 230 syntax declarations;
+- 700 rules, of which 461 are equational and 239 operational;
+- five contexts;
+- one configuration;
+- one claim;
+- 149 function-tagged declarations, 110 totals, 30 priority rules, 32 concrete
+  rules, 26 `owise` rules, 22 opaque/no-evaluator symbols;
+- zero simplification rules and zero `functional` declarations.
+
+Each of the 937 rows records exact source/line, module, attributes/class,
+normalized text, and whether it is reachable from the submitted AST. The
+inventory regenerates byte-identically with SHA-256
+`3bcf9ba27837a5c1d9a34dd6a69f26dc5c90b736f9eacd4b4b00564e26eb2ac1`;
+see [`evidence/05_inventory.py`](evidence/05_inventory.py) and
+[`evidence/05_inventory_command.log`](evidence/05_inventory_command.log).
+
+All reference-semantics rows are fixed selected semantics because that tree is
+byte-identical to the trusted supplied baseline. Unreachable rows cannot affect
+this theorem. The 22 opaque float, sorting, and MD5 symbols are all unreachable.
+No opaque or fresh value affects a branch, state cell, return, exception, or
+postcondition.
+
+The full construct mapping and the individual decision for each candidate-local
+record are in
+[`evidence/05_static_review.md`](evidence/05_static_review.md). The used path
+preserves:
+
+- left-to-right expression and argument evaluation;
+- scope lookup and builtins fallthrough;
+- exact unbounded integer arithmetic;
+- positive-divisor Python `%` and `//`;
+- callee frame allocation, parameter binding, assignment, return, restoration,
+  and deallocation;
+- the value and all material state cells.
+
+### Candidate-local definitions
+
+`decimalDigit` and `decimalDigitSum` are deterministic definitional summaries,
+not execution replacements. Their guards cover every use. The place-1 and
+higher-place rules are disjoint, and the five places are exact on 0..10000.
+Their `[total]` attributes leave uncovered, unused arguments underspecified but
+do not assign a false value used by this theorem.
+
+`binaryNumeral(N) => str(binCodes(N))` names the supplied semantics' binary
+payload. The theorem uses it only for the nonnegative digit sum. It does not
+replace the program's `bin` call.
+
+### Prefix-slice operational bridge
+
+`verification.k:31-38` rewrites:
+
+`Subscript(str(iCons(48,iCons(98,REST))), Slice(Int(2),NoBound,NoBound))`
+
+to `str(REST)` at priority 40.
+
+Complete context review:
+
+- it accepts an arbitrary K continuation and frames every other cell;
+- its operands and literal bound are already fixed, so no lookup/binding choice
+  or side-effecting evaluation is skipped;
+- both the fixed path and bridge read/change only `<k>`;
+- it introduces no abrupt control, return, exception, frame operation,
+  allocation, output, or state change;
+- fixed slice start is 2, default stop is the sequence length, and default step
+  is 1, which yields exactly the tail `REST`;
+- priority preempts only that longer equivalent path.
+
+A bridge-free Haskell definition importing `MPY` (not `VERIFICATION`) proves
+three representative ground identities and all 37 payloads reachable from
+digit sums 0..36. Every claim quantifies over arbitrary `CONT:K`, so continuation
+containment is tested rather than assumed. Commands exit 0 and print `#Top`:
+
+- [`evidence/04_slice_ground.k`](evidence/04_slice_ground.k)
+- [`evidence/04_slice_ground.log`](evidence/04_slice_ground.log)
+- [`evidence/04_slice_reachable.k`](evidence/04_slice_reachable.k)
+- [`evidence/04_slice_reachable.log`](evidence/04_slice_reachable.log)
+
+A stronger bridge-free theorem quantified over every symbolic `REST:IntSeq`
+does not close. Its residual is the unreduced recursive `buildIS` identity, not
+a conflicting result; exact command and exit 1 are preserved in
+[`evidence/04_validate_slice_bridge.log`](evidence/04_validate_slice_bridge.log).
+Ordinary structural induction establishes the identity for all finite
+`IntSeq`s, and no false-conclusion witness exists. Per the required decision
+rule, this is recorded as a narrower evidence gap, not labeled unsound.
+
+There is no answer-encoding axiom, unconstrained result oracle, call/body
+bypass, conflicting equation overlap, unsafe priority effect, or fabricated
+semantics for a used construct.
+
+Stage result: sound for the target theorem, with a universal
+connection-theorem auditability concern.
+
+## 6. Fresh non-vacuity test
+
+The reviewer created a distinct mutation
+[`evidence/06_spec_vacuity.k`](evidence/06_spec_vacuity.k). It changes only the
+result obligation from:
+
+`binaryNumeral(decimalDigitSum(N))`
+
+to the deliberately false:
+
+`binaryNumeral(decimalDigitSum(N) +Int 1)`.
+
+`N=0` satisfies the original precondition. The real result is `"0"` while the
+mutation requires `"1"`.
+
+The exact dry-run command exits 0, establishing successful parsing/building.
+The actual `kprove` command exits 1, emits `WarnStuckClaimState`, and reports the
+failed equality between the real `binCodes(digit_sum)` and off-by-one payload.
+The failure is therefore the expected unmet result obligation, not a parser
+error, missing import, timeout, unrelated crash, or unreachable mutation.
+
+Artifacts:
+
+- [`evidence/06_nonvacuity.sh`](evidence/06_nonvacuity.sh)
+- [`evidence/06_nonvacuity.log`](evidence/06_nonvacuity.log)
+- [`evidence/06_vacuity_proof_raw.log`](evidence/06_vacuity_proof_raw.log)
+
+Stage result: pass.
+
+## 7. Proven versus assumed accounting
+
+### Machine-checked result
+
+Under the supplied MPY semantics plus the reviewed candidate-local definitions
+and exact slice identity, for every K integer `N` with `0 <= N <= 10000`, the
+actual submitted translated module and call reach a final configuration whose
+returned value is the binary numeral of the sum of `N`'s decimal digits, with
+the pinned observable state restored/preserved. This is a partial-correctness
+reachability result; it is not a general theorem about inputs outside the
+precondition or arbitrary Python.
+
+### Trust ledger
+
+| Boundary | Dependents/effect | Assessment |
+|---|---|---|
+| Trusted supplied `reference-semantics` tree | All execution, cells, control, integer/builtin/slice behavior | Authorized fixed semantics; integrity checked byte-for-byte. Its unrelated opaque operations are outside the used path. |
+| K 7.1.337 compiler, Haskell/LLVM backends, SMT/native `Int`, `Bool`, `String`, `Map`, and `List` hooks | Proof closure and concrete execution | Standard low-level proof trust boundary; acceptable and explicitly versioned. |
+| Trusted `/reference/py2mpy.py` | Python-to-MPY bridge | Candidate translator matches it; regenerated MPY is byte-identical. |
+| Trusted `/reference/canonical.py` and prompt | Natural-language intent oracle | Exhaustive finite-domain differential gives zero mismatches. This supports implementation-to-intent alignment; it is not substituted for the K proof. |
+| `decimalDigit`, `decimalDigitSum`, `binaryNumeral` | Formal postcondition only | Truthful guarded definitions, independently checked over the entire intended finite domain; no program body is bypassed. |
+| Prefix-slice priority rule | Final returned string and target closure | Semantics-preserving by structural reasoning and bridge-free exhaustive reachable-payload proofs. Candidate lacks a universal connection theorem; concern, not a false-rule witness. |
+| Entry `<exit-code>` omission | Exit code is arbitrary but preserved | Non-material here: no used rule changes it, and a normal exit-code-0 witness satisfies the entry. |
+| Termination/out-of-domain behavior | Excluded | Only partial correctness is claimed; negative values and values above 10000 are outside the theorem. |
+
+The differential tests and summary tests support only the finite
+implementation/intent and equation bridges they exercise. They are not used as
+a substitute for the independently reconstructed `kprove #Top`.
+
+### Final decision
+
+The proof is reconstructed, non-vacuous, result-constraining, and pins the real
+generated program. No material soundness or adequacy defect and no false-rule
+witness was found. Missing provenance files and the absent universal
+bridge-connection artifact prevent an unqualified pass but do not make a false
+claim provable on the intended domain.
+
+VERDICT: CONCERNS
+LEGITIMACY: LEGIT
